<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Ordered basis upper triangular</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Let \[ A = {@AA@}. \]</p>
<p>Find an ordered basis \((v_1, v_2, v_3)\) of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) with respect to which the matrix of \(A\) is upper triangular. That is, the matrix \(P\) with columns \(v_1, v_2, v_3\) is invertible and \(P^{-1}AP\) is upper triangular.</p>
<p>Enter the basis as a list of column vectors in square brackets, in order, for example <code>[c(1,2,3), c(4,5,6), c(7,8,9)]</code>.</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>\(P^{-1}AP\) is upper triangular exactly when \(A v_1\) is a multiple of \(v_1\), \(A v_2\) lies in \(\operatorname{span}(v_1, v_2)\), and so on. So \(v_1\) must be an eigenvector of \(A\), and the order of the vectors matters.</p>
<p>One answer is \({@ta1@}\): with \(P\) the matrix with these columns, \[ P^{-1}AP = {@UU@}. \] Reversing the order of these vectors gives a lower triangular matrix instead.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber/>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2026111900</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>stack_include_contrib("validators.mac");
stack_include_contrib("matrix.mac");
validate_veclist3(ex) := validate_veclist(ex, 3);
/* Build backwards: A = P.U.P^^-1 with U upper triangular, so the columns of P, in order, are an answer. */
/* P has determinant 1, so A has integer entries; over every choice the largest entry of A is 10. */
PP: rand([matrix([1,1,0],[0,1,1],[0,0,1]), matrix([1,0,0],[1,1,0],[0,1,1]), matrix([1,0,1],[0,1,0],[1,1,2])]);
dd: rand_selection([-2,-1,1,2], 3);
/* Non-zero entries above the diagonal, so that reversing the order never gives an upper-triangular matrix. */
UU: matrix([dd[1], rand([-1,1]), rand([-1,1])], [0, dd[2], rand([-1,1])], [0, 0, dd[3]]);
AA: PP . UU . PP^^-1;
ta1: makelist(apply(c, list_matrix_entries(col(PP, k))), k, 1, 3);</text>
    </questionvariables>
